# 20. Responsive Breakpoints

Target:

### Mobile

< 640px

Rules:

- one-column layout
- bottom action bar where useful
- large touch targets
- no hover-dependent functionality
- compact controls

### Tablet

640px–1023px

Rules:

- flexible two-column layout where space permits
- editor controls can become a drawer/sheet

### Desktop

≥ 1024px

Rules:

- preview + tools side-by-side
- keyboard shortcuts
- larger workspace

Do not design for fixed device widths.

Use fluid layouts.

---

# 21. Accessibility

Minimum requirements:

- semantic HTML
- keyboard navigation
- visible focus state
- accessible labels
- sufficient contrast
- alt text
- no color-only status indication
- buttons must have clear labels
- touch targets approximately 44px or larger
- screen-reader friendly status messages

Processing status should use an accessible live region.

Example:

> Removing background. Please wait.

---

# 22. Performance Requirements

## 22.1 Core UI

The landing page should load quickly before the heavy AI model is requested.

Do NOT download the AI model immediately when the user opens the website.

Instead:

```text
Open website
 ↓
Lightweight UI
 ↓
User uploads image
 ↓
User clicks Remove Background
 ↓
Load AI runtime/model
```

This is critical.

## 22.2 Code splitting

Lazy-load:

- AI runtime
- AI model
- advanced editor
- batch processing
- object removal

The user should not download 100% of the application just to crop an image.

## 22.3 Web Worker

Move heavy processing away from the main UI thread where possible.

Goal:

The UI should remain responsive while AI inference runs.

---

# 23. Privacy

The default product promise:

> **Your images stay on your device whenever local processing is available.**

Do not claim "100% private" unless the implementation and analytics architecture truly support it.

MVP:

- no image uploads
- no image database
- no image history on server
- no authentication required
- no third-party image-processing API

If analytics are added later, use privacy-conscious analytics and clearly disclose them.

---

# 24. Error Handling

Common errors:

### Unsupported format

> This file format isn't supported. Try JPG, PNG, or WebP.

### File too large

> This image is too large. Try an image under 20 MB.

### Browser memory problem

> Your device ran out of memory while processing this image. Try a smaller image.

### WebGPU unavailable

> GPU acceleration isn't available. Falling back to CPU processing.

### AI model failed

> We couldn't process this image. Please try again or use a smaller image.

Never show raw technical errors to normal users.

