# 29. MVP Definition of Done

The MVP is considered complete when:

- [ ] User can open the website without logging in.
- [ ] User can upload JPG/PNG/WebP.
- [ ] User can preview the image.
- [ ] User can remove background.
- [ ] User can choose transparent/solid background.
- [ ] User can resize the result.
- [ ] User can crop the result.
- [ ] User can export PNG/JPG/WebP.
- [ ] Application works on desktop.
- [ ] Application works on mobile.
- [ ] Application does not require a paid API.
- [ ] Image processing happens locally for supported features.
- [ ] Heavy AI assets are lazy-loaded.
- [ ] Errors are understandable.
- [ ] GitHub repository is public.
- [ ] README explains setup and architecture.
- [ ] License is included.
- [ ] Application deploys successfully to Vercel.
- [ ] Application can also be deployed to Netlify.
- [ ] No API keys are exposed in the frontend.

---

# 30. Success Metrics

For the first public release:

### Product

- Background removal succeeds on common portrait/object images.
- Exported image maintains expected dimensions.
- No visible watermark.
- No login required.

### Performance

Target:

- landing page should feel fast
- AI model should not load until needed
- UI should remain responsive during processing
- mobile should degrade gracefully

### Developer

- clean TypeScript
- reusable components
- understandable architecture
- no unnecessary dependencies
- documented model licensing

---

# 31. Security Considerations

Because files are user-controlled:

- validate file MIME type
- validate file size
- do not trust file extension alone
- avoid injecting filename into HTML
- revoke object URLs when no longer needed
- avoid unnecessary server uploads
- do not expose API secrets
- sanitize any user-generated metadata
- keep dependencies updated

If a server is introduced later:

- validate uploads server-side
- limit request size
- rate limit expensive processing
- never store images by default
- delete temporary files automatically

---

# 32. Recommended Browser Strategy

Priority:

```text
1. WebGPU
      ↓
2. WASM / CPU
      ↓
3. Inform user if device is insufficient
```

For non-AI operations:

```text
Canvas / OffscreenCanvas
```

For heavy AI:

```text
Web Worker
+
ONNX Runtime Web
+
WebGPU/WASM
```

---

# 33. What NOT to Build First

Do not start with:

- authentication
- database
- admin dashboard
- payment
- subscriptions
- cloud image storage
- social login
- complicated editor layers
- object removal
- 4× upscaling
- batch processing

Start with:

```text
Upload
 ↓
Preview
 ↓
Remove Background
 ↓
Edit Background
 ↓
Resize/Crop
 ↓
Download
```

This is the shortest path to a usable product.

---

# 34. First Development Task

The first coding milestone should be:

## "PixelForge UI Shell"

Before integrating any AI:

1. Create GitHub repository.
2. Create Next.js project.
3. Install Tailwind CSS.
4. Install shadcn/ui.
5. Create responsive landing page.
6. Create upload component.
7. Implement local image preview.
8. Create editor layout.
9. Add dark/light theme.
10. Test desktop/tablet/mobile.
11. Deploy to Vercel.
12. Confirm production build works.

Only after this succeeds should the AI model be integrated.

---

# 35. Initial Commands

Recommended starting point:

```bash
npx create-next-app@latest pixelforge --typescript --eslint --app
cd pixelforge
npm run dev
```

Then configure Tailwind CSS according to the current Next.js/Tailwind documentation.

Then initialize shadcn/ui.

Install the image/AI dependencies only when their corresponding feature is started.

For ONNX Runtime Web:

```bash
npm install onnxruntime-web
```

Do not install a large collection of image libraries on day one.

